{"name":"Sheer Blouse","hex":"#e4d0c3","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sheer","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Very thin or transparent.","Pure in composition; unmixed; unadulterated."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Clean; completely; at once."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sheer"},{"term":"blouse","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A shirt, typically loose and reaching from the neck to the waist.","A shirt for women or girls, particularly a shirt with buttons and often a collar; a dress shirt tailored for women."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To hang a garment in loose folds.","To tuck one's pants/trousers (into one's boots)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blouse"}],"entries":[{"title":"Blouse","blocks":[{"text":"A blouse is a loose-fitting upper garment, mainly worn by workmen, peasants, artists, women, and children. It is typically gathered at the waist or hips so that it hangs loosely (\"blouses\") over the wearer's body. Today, the word most commonly refers to a girl's or woman's dress shirt, although there is considerable confusion between a true blouse and a women's shirt. It can also refer to a man's shirt if it is a loose-fitting style, though it rarely is. Traditionally, the term has been used to refer to a shirt which blouses out or (alternatively) has an unmistakably feminine appearance; in contemporary fashion, standard dress shirts of both genders commonly come in such a loose fit without being considered \"blouses\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Striped_Bow_Neck_Blouse_and_a_Navy_Blue_Mini_Skirt_%2818966025833%29.jpg/330px-Striped_Bow_Neck_Blouse_and_a_Navy_Blue_Mini_Skirt_%2818966025833%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blouse"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Poet-shirt.jpg/330px-Poet-shirt.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Poet-shirt.jpg/330px-Poet-shirt.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A man and woman wearing poet shirts—a unisex blouse, inspired by Romanticism and worn by fashionable people such as the English poet, Lord Byron","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APoet-shirt.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Puffy_Shirt_2006.jpg/500px-Puffy_Shirt_2006.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Puffy_Shirt_2006.jpg/500px-Puffy_Shirt_2006.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Seinfeld \"puffy shirt\", worn by Jerry Seinfeld, is an example of a poet shirt blouse.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APuffy_Shirt_2006.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Garibaldi_and_his_wife%2C_Anita%2C_defending_Rome_in_1849.jpg/250px-Garibaldi_and_his_wife%2C_Anita%2C_defending_Rome_in_1849.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c9/Garibaldi_and_his_wife%2C_Anita%2C_defending_Rome_in_1849.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Garibaldi_and_his_wife%2C_Anita%2C_defending_Rome_in_1849.jpg/250px-Garibaldi_and_his_wife%2C_Anita%2C_defending_Rome_in_1849.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Giuseppe Garibaldi (center), the Italian patriot, and his wife Anita (right), popularized the red Garibaldi shirt, a type of military blouse which became popular as early 1860s civilian fashion with both men and women in Europe and North America.","l